gO1(){return"Authority and command"},
gO6(){return"The flower of attraction \u2014 charm, romance and the notice of others. It is read from the day branch's trine, and the branch it names is where that charm attaches. Absent, attraction is simply not one of the chart's defining threads."},
gO3(){return"The helper who arrives when needed: mentors, patrons, the door that opens at the right moment. It is the one star read from a pair of branches rather than from one. Where it falls is where support tends to come from."},
gO9(){return"The post horse \u2014 journeys, relocation, and a restlessness that will not sit still. It faces the opening of the day branch's trine, so it marks movement outward. In the year pillar it reads as distance from where one began; in the hour, as movement late in life."},
gNV(){return"The star of study, writing and examinations. Read from the day stem, it points at the branch where learning comes easily. Its presence favours scholarship and clear expression rather than luck."},
gNY(){return"The canopy over the carriage: art, philosophy and a taste for solitude. It sits on the closing earth branch of the day branch's trine. Traditionally it marks someone set slightly apart, whether by temperament or by calling."},
gO0(){return"The general at the centre of the trine \u2014 authority, command, and the ability to hold a position. It falls on the branch the day branch's group turns around, and lends weight to the pillar it occupies."},
